e swathes of the country  now where women would have to travel  great distances and probably a great  expense  to have an abortion that in my view does  endanger lives it does cause real  concern and misery for many many people  and i think it's up to congress now the  legislature in washington to actually  get on and do the job that of course we  did in our house of commons and  parliament 50 years ago with the  abortion act and we will come to that  but i just want to uh unpick a little  bit more about what's happening in the  united states a lot of laws and  precedence of course in the us are  interlinked  even acknowledged by uh conservative  justices look at this one clarence  thomas in future cases we should  reconsider all of the court's  substantive due process precedence  including griswold which is granting  rights to contraception lawrence that's  decriminalizing um sodomy and uh  obergefell i think legalizing same-sex  marriage what does that actually mean  well  it's about a big argument between  different views of the u.s constitution  uh judges like clarence thomas  conservative judges take a much more  strict interpretation of the  constitution and don't like to see it  being stretched in a way that they think  defies its true meaning now there are  other judges so-called liberal judges  who take different views and want to  make sure that our society involves the  constitution involves with it that's one  of the perils k of having a written  constitution which of course was  originally written what 250 years ago  amendments this particular member the  14th amendment is the one that they  really obsess about passed just after  the u.s civil war it will tell you about  how a written constitution can be a  wonderful thing but it can also really  be a minefield for lawyers and a field  day for for the sort of arguments that  we see developing in the u.s so so where  is that leaving us robert as far as  things like same-sex marriage are  concerned in the