stance back in 2017 he says he's  completely opposed to abortion  abortion is morally indefensible life is  sacrosanct and begins at the point of  conception and asks whether he uh  opposes whether the opposition i should  say applies in cases of pregnancies  resulting from rape or incest he said  i'm afraid so if you have people with  views like that in government is there  any person um who feels that you know it  even if you've been raped  or incest you still must go ahead with  the pregnancy well i i don't agree with  jacob about that i do believe that life  begins at the point of conception but i  do think that to ask a woman who's been  the victim of a horrific crime of rape  and having prosecuted many of those  crimes and met victims i have had  first-hand experience of what it does to  people  i don't think it's right for us to sit  in judgment  and in effect to force people into a  situation that was not their choice and  the important point here is that of  course jacob is a member of the  government we serve together in cabinet  it is a conscience issue for mps it's  not a party issue when we vote on these  issues in the house of commons it's on  our individual consciences and i think  that that's that's the right system to  have it means that uh the pressures of  the whips disappear and that one is left  alone thinking about these things guided  perhaps by the views of constituents and  others i i one is able to make a  considered opinion it's jake and i do  not agree on that however there are many  other issues that we do agree on uh and  um you know our moral consciences will  hopefully guide us when we uh may cast  those important votes yeah there will be  some concern though from viewers  watching this morning that if jacob  reese openly says what he said there  will be others within government within  cabinets who will agree with him but not  potentially as openly as he has spoken  for now um should they be at all  concerned people watching at home 