uld be a very retrograde step i think  she might be referring to the british  bill of rights uh the human rights act  is set uh to be replaced with a british  bill of rights that's certainly what uh  the now justice secretary was looking at  last time he was on the programme  is your government  i don't know whether you agree not  allowing a loosening of human rights in  the uk  i don't think it is i think that many of  the changes in that bill are ones that i  would have brought forward because i  started the process of review and  updating of the human rights act which  is now over 20 years of age i think  there's a risk that we start confusing  things by uh in effect defining certain  issues whether it's jury trial which i  strongly believe in having prosecuted  and defended  cases  whether by enshrining and elevating  those particular rights people are going  to say well if you're doing it for that  why aren't you doing it for something  else and i think this bill is going to  be vulnerable to a number of amendments  on that basis and as conservatives as a  conservative i think we've got to be  very careful  about preserving our common law our  statute law and the unwritten  constitution of the uk and i don't want  to see a sort of you know  domestic set of rights that we create  with good intentions that only so more  confusion  so what you're suggesting is by not  having the constitution like they have  in the united states that that makes us  actually freer rather than more limited  i think so i think the tradition  in our country is that we've been free  to do  everything that's not prohibited by law  if it's like a cricket pitch where  you've got the boundary which is defined  which is where the law  sits and within that we are free to act  as you know free citizens under the law  i think that's a far better system than  having prescribed rights that can be  interpreted by let's face it unelected  judges uh who then are drawn into the  political arena in a 